WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.100
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.939
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:12.090
Last year there were over 4 billion passengers
in airlines around the world, a figure that

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.620
grew from about 2.5 billion just 10 years
earlier.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:21.439
The airline industry is big business with
a total revenue of 834 billion dollars expected

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:22.439
in 2018.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:27.339
[1] With this kind of money for taking governments
and private companies want to get their share

00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:31.699
by designing an airport that can facilitate
AND encourage air traffic to pass through

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:32.699
it.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.660
But if we take a look at the footprints of
some of the busiest airports in the world,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.750
there are some patterns, but nothing immediately
jumps as the go to design for air traffic.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.800
So let’s demystify some of this mysterious
world of aviation and figure out how to optimally

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.580
design an airport.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.120
In the early days of aviation runways we often
nothing more than a cleared field.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.280
The Wright Brothers choose Kitty Hawk, an
isolated strip of beach, because it had plenty

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.650
of space and more importantly strong winds
to help get their planes off the ground.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.190
Today, that practice isn’t all that different.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.560
Airports are some of the largest plots of
land allocated for a single use in any city,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.130
and wind still dictates their design.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:14.820
Once again, taking a look at runways around
the world, you may not see a pattern at first,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:19.010
but if you overlay the prevailing winds in
their area the pattern becomes clear.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.670
[2] Most airports in the Northern Hemisphere,
are alined east to west, which coincides with

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.920
the most consistent wind directions.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.990
Inspect at any airport and it’s likely they
have followed this design principle.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.810
This is done to take advantage of the wind,
just as the Wright brothers did all that time

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:39.530
ago, because a head on wind adds lift reducing
the power required for take-off, and reduces

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.750
landing speed.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.450
It also maximises the operational hours of
the airport in windy conditions.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.280
The alternative is landing with heavy crosswinds,
which is not particularly fun for the passengers

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.310
or easy for the pilot, if the pilot can land
at all.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.090
The crosswinds a plane can tolerate differ
with plane design, with planes with larger

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.909
vertical surfaces like winglets and vertical
stabilizers being more susceptible to crosswinds

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:03.380
pushing them off course.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.560
A typical plane like a Boeing 737, the most
common airliner on the planet, can tolerate

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.040
a crosswind of about 60 km/h [3] with a dry
runway and 55 on wet.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.770
Anything exceeding that and planes need to
hold until winds calm down, or divert to an

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.849
alternative airport.

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:25.980
Tailwinds are even less tolerable with winds
from 18-25 km/h making it too dangerous to

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.750
land at any, but the longest runways.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.640
Some Airports, like London City Airport, have
to enforce their own crosswind tolerances

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.160
below plane tolerances, as their runways are
narrower than average.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.470
Fortunately tailwinds are easy to counteract
by landing in the opposite direction.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.160
NATs, formerly known as ‘National Air Traffic
Services’ , illustrated how these shifting

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.200
winds affect air traffic with UK Air Traffic
data from February 14th 2014.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:58.770
On that day winds of up to 110 km/h were recorded,
making it impossible for aircraft to land

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:03.680
all over the UK, causing towering holding
stacks to open over London airspace, with

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.819
the lower aircraft waiting for a break in
the wind to land.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:11.030
Yellow flight paths here are delayed planes,
which were approaching two hours, red flight

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.660
paths are diverted planes.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.050
This is an extreme case, but this incident
cost these airports and airlines massive amounts

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.230
of money.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.920
Designers of airports will analyse decades
of wind data to minimise any possible operational

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.310
shutdowns like this.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.310
[4]

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:28.849
This is our first design principle to maximise
traffic, to simply minimise shutdowns due

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:29.860
to wind.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.910
Now that we have chosen our runway direction,
let’s pick a location in our city to place

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.150
our airport.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.580
With this wind alignment in mind, let’s
say East to West for this example, most city

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.960
airports will attempt to place the airport
on the Northern or Southern edge of the city,

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.780
so low flying aircraft coming in to land don’t
have to fly over the city.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.940
This is the case for most airports.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:54.819
But Heathrow airport is a special little butterfly
located smack in the middle of London.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:59.709
This is fantastic for accessibility with London
city centre only a short train ride away,

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:01.159
but it creates problems of its own.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:02.720
The first is noise.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.599
In the 1950s the owners of Heathrow signed
an agreement with the residents of Cranford

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:11.800
to not allow planes to take off to the east,
which is often needed as the wind blows from

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.830
the East about 30% of the time in London.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:19.120
This was done to reduce noise over the most
populated area neighbouring Heathrow.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.350
This agreement is no longer in place, but
it still affects how Heathrow operates.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.190
It runs a policy of runway alternation.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:31.030
From 6 am to 3 pm, planes will land on the
Northern runway and take-off from the Southern

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.030
Runway.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:36.669
Then the moment the clock strikes 3 they switch,
with planes taking off from the Northern runway

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:38.280
and landing on the South.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.940
This order also flips every second week.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.690
All of this is done to give the residents
around Heathrow some relief from the constant

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:47.420
blaring of jet engines over their homes.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.020
Not an ideal situation when trying to run
a busy airport.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.020
[5]

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.840
Parallel runways like this are great for traffic,
as two planes can land and take-off simultaneously.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.410
Once again maximising traffic.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.040
You can see two planes landing at the same
time at Heathrow, typically between 6 am and

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.960
7 am when departures are quiet, but you do
need space between the runways The FAA specifies

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.430
that parallel runways with centrelines spaced
760 to 1300 metres apart must use staggered

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.270
approaches, meaning planes cannot land side
by side Runways with centre lines spaced between

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:26.680
1300 metres and 2700 metres can land simultaneously
with air traffic control monitoring.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.320
Seeing a flight land alongside your own is
a pretty common sight at LAX for this reason

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.310
with it’s runway pairs 1.4 kilometres apart,

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:39.290
and even though Gatwick Airport has two runways
it operates as a single use runway as they

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.030
are too close to each other to work simultaneously.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:46.900
The alternative to parallel runways are intersecting
runways, and while these are more space efficient,

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.190
and can provide alternative approaches with
a shift wind patterns, they come with their

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:56.230
own risks and require careful monitoring by
air traffic control to prevent crashes.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:00.530
In general a single runway operating both
take-offs and landings can achieve a similar

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:04.000
throughput of aircraft if wind conditions
are favourable. [6]

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.620
So when looking to increase air traffic volumes,
placing additional parallel runways at least

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.240
1.3 kilometres apart is best.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.830
This is where Heathrow runs into its next
design issue.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:17.140
It’s location has made it near impossible
to expand.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:22.949
Heathrow is now operating at 98% capacity,
and being the UK’s hub international airport

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.380
increasing capacity is a major concern.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.500
So where can we place another runway?

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.660
Let’s see.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:35.139
Hmmm nope, no, nope, definitely not, that
won’t work…..or will it.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:39.910
Amazingly this was the proposal set forth
earlier this year that will require a village

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:43.760
to be bulldozed and a tunnel dug to reconnect
the M25.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:50.009
This will cost 3.3 billion dollars for compulsory
land purchases alone, with a further 18.4

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.680
billion for the expansion itself, though the
British Government has promised this bill

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.820
will be entirely privately funded.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:56.820
[7]

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:01.150
Under its current format, Heathrow is constrained
to about 480,000 flights a year, but they

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:05.180
have managed to continually grow passenger
numbers by increasing the numbers of large

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:09.880
long haul flights passing through it, but
this is not an option for all airports, as

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.740
their runways are too short.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.710
Take Dublin airport as an example, it currently
operates two intersecting runways.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:22.130
One 2623 metres long and another 2072 metres
long.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.900
To see why this is a problem let’s analyse
runway length requirements.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:31.000
Basic runway length is determined by airplane
performance, and to calculate it we analyse

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.880
the critical moments in an aeroplanes take-off
sequence.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.940
A plane hits 6 critical speeds during take-off.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:41.139
The first is the stall speed, this is the
minimum speed at which a plane will remain

00:07:41.139 --> 00:07:42.139
airborne.

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:46.300
This is not used as the take-off speed, as
any decrease in speed due to fluctuations

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.350
in wind or orientation of the plane will cause
the plane to fall.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:54.810
The next critical speed is the minimum control
speed, this applies to multi-engined aircraft

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:55.810
only.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.979
If a multi-engined aircraft loses an engine,
the uneven thrust between the wings will cause

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:03.139
the plane to turn, this is called yaw in aviation.

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:07.449
To counteract this the rudder will be deflected
to provide the opposite yawing moment.

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:11.941
The rudder needs air passing over it to work,
and thus the minimum control velocity is the

00:08:11.941 --> 00:08:16.509
velocity at which the rudder can provide enough
of a yawing moment to keep the plane straight

00:08:16.509 --> 00:08:18.539
in the event of an engine failure.

00:08:18.539 --> 00:08:21.060
The next speed a pilot needs to worry about
is V1.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.940
V1 is a line in the sand for pilots making
a decision whether to abort a take-off.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:31.400
If something happens before V1, like an engine
failure, the pilot must abort the take-off.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.860
If it happens above V1, they must continue
with the take-off, as it would be unsafe to

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:36.860
stop.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.469
This is the most important speed for runway
designers.

00:08:39.469 --> 00:08:43.860
At this speed the plane will need enough distance
on the runway to safely bring the plane to

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:48.200
a stop, which is exactly the same as the distance
needed to reach V1.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.779
The resulting total runway length is thus
called the balanced field length.

00:08:52.779 --> 00:08:54.209
Back to that in a moment.

00:08:54.209 --> 00:08:59.190
The 4th critical speed is Vr, or the rotation
speed, this is the point the plane can begin

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:01.869
to lift its nose up and begin it’s ascent.

00:09:01.869 --> 00:09:06.149
The next speed, which results in some of the
coolest testing videos, is the minimum unstick

00:09:06.149 --> 00:09:10.800
speed, Vmu, this is the speed the plane can
take-off at its maximum pitch, which is actually

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.619
the point where the tail skid hits the ground.

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:16.420
This is a video of a test pilot testing this
speed.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.740
Since this would be incredibly uncomfortable,
the actual take off-speed is at least 10%

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.309
higher than the minimum unstick speed.

00:09:23.309 --> 00:09:27.459
At this point no part of the plane is touching
the ground, and it is officially airborne.

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:32.449
It must then accelerate to it’s climb speed
V2, which it must achieve with a minimum clearance

00:09:32.449 --> 00:09:34.619
of 10 metres from any obstacle.

00:09:34.619 --> 00:09:38.149
With all this in mind we can begin designing
our runway length.

00:09:38.149 --> 00:09:42.139
Planes are typically designed to use standard
runway lengths, and not the other way around,

00:09:42.139 --> 00:09:45.959
but these speeds can vary between different
aircraft, so let’s start our calculation

00:09:45.959 --> 00:09:48.620
with the world’s largest plane the Airbus
a380.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:53.860
Here we will be assuming a maximum take-off
weight at sea-level with the international

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.399
standard atmosphere model for weather conditions,
and no wind.

00:09:57.399 --> 00:10:04.619
A typical decision speed of a fully laden
a380 is about 280 km/h, or about 78 metres

00:10:04.619 --> 00:10:05.619
per second.

00:10:05.619 --> 00:10:09.500
This, along with other critical speeds, do
vary with flight conditions and will vary

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.160
for the runway itself.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.899
The pilots have a flight computer to output
the relevant critical speeds for this reason,

00:10:14.899 --> 00:10:19.309
and gives them an appropriate thrust percentage
to provide the acceleration needed.

00:10:19.309 --> 00:10:21.069
This is just an example.

00:10:21.069 --> 00:10:25.470
Assuming an average acceleration of about
2 metres per second squared we can calculate

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:30.220
the distance needed to reach v1 by employing
one of the fundamental kinematics equations

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.490
every high school student learned in physics,
specifically this one.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:38.809
Initial velocity is zero and we can rearrange
the equation to find distance.

00:10:38.809 --> 00:10:43.649
Applying our variables and we get a distance
of 1521 metres to reach v1.

00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:48.110
In the event of an aborted take-off the plane
will need an equal distance to bring the plane

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:51.360
to a stop, this is called the balanced field
length.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.509
Which is simply double this distance at 3042
metres.

00:10:55.509 --> 00:11:00.739
Again this value varies wildly and v1 is dictated
by the runway available, not just the plane

00:11:00.739 --> 00:11:01.819
performance.

00:11:01.819 --> 00:11:07.019
This graph provided by airbus, shows the various
runway lengths needed for the a380 at various

00:11:07.019 --> 00:11:11.249
take off weights and altitudes, and agrees
roughly with our calculation [8]

00:11:11.249 --> 00:11:15.769
So, as you can see, Dublin Airport’s runways
are too short to accommodate fully laden planes

00:11:15.769 --> 00:11:17.170
like this.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:21.769
Large long haul planes can and do land here
when needed, but cannot take-off with a full

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:26.209
tank of fuel and passengers on board, which
prevents any large long haul carriers from

00:11:26.209 --> 00:11:27.619
operating from Dublin.

00:11:27.619 --> 00:11:32.759
Thus a new runway is being built to run parallel
to the existing longer runway to the South,

00:11:32.759 --> 00:11:34.309
but even this may be too short.

00:11:34.309 --> 00:11:39.269
As winds and weather will increase the runway
distance needed, on top of this Dublin airport

00:11:39.269 --> 00:11:44.629
is 75 metres above sea level, which would
add about 2% to runway length requirements,

00:11:44.629 --> 00:11:49.709
as the thinner air reduces the lift provided
by the wings, and thus increases the take-off

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:50.709
speeds.

00:11:50.709 --> 00:11:59.899
The longest runway in the world in Tibet at
an elevation of 4334 metres or 14,219 feet

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:01.790
is 5.5 kilometres long for this reason.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:06.029
The temperature of the air at the airport
also has a significant effect on runway length

00:12:06.029 --> 00:12:11.259
requirements, with an additional 1% of runway
length required for every 1 degree celsius

00:12:11.259 --> 00:12:16.029
over the standard atmosphere measurement we
used earlier at 15 degrees celsius.

00:12:16.029 --> 00:12:20.839
Once again this is a result of reduced air
density reducing lift capabilities.

00:12:20.839 --> 00:12:24.699
Last year this actually resulted in flights
being delayed and cancelled out of Phoenix

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:29.179
Arizona when temperatures rocketed to 49 degrees
celsius.

00:12:29.179 --> 00:12:32.920
Clearly, designing airports is a tricky and
expensive business.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:37.830
If money and space wasn’t an issue the ideal
design would simply be multiple parallel runways

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.649
spaced about 1.3 kilometres apart.

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:46.230
The busiest airport in the world the Atlanta
International Airport runs 5 parallel runways.Beijing

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:51.540
comes next, running 3 parallel runways each
far enough apart to run simultaneous operations,

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:53.839
and long enough to accomodate any plane.

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:58.860
Dubai Airport coming 3rd with it’s two parallel
runnings each over 4000 metres long due to

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.989
the heat of Dubai, allowing it to be one of
the world’s most important stop over points

00:13:02.989 --> 00:13:04.819
for long haul carriers.

00:13:04.819 --> 00:13:07.639
This pattern reoccurs all over the world.

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:11.170
International airports with parallel runways
long enough to accomodate large planes are

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:15.679
consistently the busiest, but with limited
space available some alternative designs have

00:13:15.679 --> 00:13:19.790
been proposed to increase capacity, like this
circular runway design.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:23.839
Which would not only be a nightmare for air
traffic control trying to direct airplanes

00:13:23.839 --> 00:13:28.100
AND make it even more difficult for a pilot
to land and take-off, but would also only

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.100
be useful in calm weather with no wind dictating
take-off direction.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:36.670
These are the kinds of issues that are only
found when engineers carefully analyze a problem.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:40.569
Without paying close attention to detail,
it’s easy to fall into the trap of thinking

00:13:40.569 --> 00:13:43.470
a design that looks promising on the surface
will work.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:47.600
And if you want to improve your own analytical
skills, a great place to start would be this

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:50.309
course on logical thinking on Brilliant.

